#{[17592186172292 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid 4x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 4}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid y - x \\ne 0}$\n3. ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$"] [17592186172301 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172302 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172303 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172304 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172305 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186172306 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172307 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186172308 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186172309 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186172375 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [17592186172376 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x+y+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.$*"] [17592186172377 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(2x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u+2,\\hspace{0.222em}u+2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{70}.$*"] [17592186172675 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$"] [17592186176191 "$\\mathbf{f}(t, z) = ⟨2\\cos t, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186176192 "$\\mathbf{f}(t, y) = ⟨2\\cos t, y, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9x^{2} + 4z^{2} = 36$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186176193 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=4{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$"] [17592186176194 "$\\mathbf{f}(x, y) = ⟨\\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, y, 9y^{2}⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x, y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9y^{2} = z$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186176195 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$"] [17592186176231 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186176311 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186176313 "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x+{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}?\n$$"] [17592186176400 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{f}(x, t) = ⟨x, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, 2\\sec t⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3z^{2} - 4y^{2} = 12$ for $z \\ge 2$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186176485 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186179958 "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}\\boxed{?}dt\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [17592186179969 "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}xy^{2}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [17592186179972 "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}4xyds,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."] [17592186180017 "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$"] [17592186180018 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186180019 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180020 "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180021 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186180022 "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180023 "![](images/0a/0ae64bf57444ac42ec5efed392471cbbac7b1e317bc1fb06b33bcd2038a2e3c1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180024 "![](images/82/82e3ae63881637f258ea92238bbcfc70f2e0993c17d74ce950d6457ffcd95039.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180025 "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=$"] [17592186180027 "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186180028 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mat